  "cli_result_text": "Submitted the 17 files that map the customer's regression across both compilers.\n\n**Key finding for the divergence:** Neither Babel's built-in legacy decorator transform (`transformer-legacy.ts`) nor its default stage-2 transform (`decorators-2018-09.ts`) nor its stage-3 transform (`decorators.ts`) ever emit `Reflect.metadata`/design-type metadata natively \u2014 Babel requires a separate third-party plugin for that (referenced even in SWC's own code comments). SWC, by contrast, bakes TS legacy-decorator metadata emission directly into its decorator pass: `decorators/legacy/mod.rs` (`TscDecorator`) conditionally runs the `Metadata`/`ParamMetadata` visitors in `decorators/legacy/metadata.rs` \u2014 but only when `emit_metadata` is `true`, which defaults to `false` (`#[serde(default)]` in `decorators/mod.rs`'s `Config`). That flag is wired from `jsc.transform.decoratorMetadata` in `crates/swc/src/config/mod.rs`, separately from `jsc.transform.legacyDecorator`.\n\nSo a customer who migrates a working `legacy: true` Babel config (paired with an external metadata plugin) to `.swcrc` with only `jsc.transform.legacyDecorator: true` set \u2014 and no `decoratorMetadata: true` \u2014 gets `@injectable()` decorators applied (via the `_ts_decorate`/`Reflect.decorate` helper) but zero `design:paramtypes`/`design:type` calls emitted. Nest's `Reflector` then reads `undefined` where it expects a metadata object, producing the reported `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading getMetadata)`. Confirmed structurally too: `swc_ecma_transforms_typescript/{typescript.rs,config.rs}` show the TS type-strip pass has no decorator awareness at all \u2014 decorators are a fully separate pass/crate in SWC, ordered by `crates/swc/src/config/mod.rs`, unlike Babel where `plugin-transform-typescript` and `plugin-proposal-decorators` are ordered plugins within the same preset pipeline.",
